   "text": {
    "L1": "An object is a thing, such as a chair. A material is what the object is made of, such as wood or plastic.",
    "L2": "The same object can use different materials, and one material can make many objects.",
    "L3": "We group materials by properties, such as transparent or opaque, hard or soft, and whether heat passes through easily.",
    "L4": "Here is a myth: handles are plastic only for colour or looks.",
    "L5": "That is not true. Plastic or wood handles have a useful property: heat does not pass through them easily, so they stay fairly cool to hold.",
    "L6": "A pan body is metal because heat passes through metal quickly and metal does not melt on the stove.",
    "L7": "So we choose a material by matching its properties to the job. For a lunchbox, steel or food-safe plastic is strong, waterproof and does not break easily.",
    "L8": "Now group materials by a property: which ones are transparent?",
    "L9": "Tap the transparent material."
   },
